<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">18 April 1521 · Worms</p><h1 class="sp-title">Luther at the Diet of Worms</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>Four years after his Ninety-five Theses against the sale of indulgences, Martin Luther had been excommunicated by Pope Leo X and summoned before the imperial diet at Worms under a promise of safe conduct. Before the young Emperor Charles V and the princes of the empire he was shown a pile of his books and asked whether he would recant them. After a night to consider, he refused.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>Unless I am convinced by the testimony of Scripture or by clear reason... I cannot and will not recant anything, for to go against conscience is neither safe nor right.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Luther's reply of 18 April 1521, translated here. The famous closing words, 'Here I stand, I can do no other', appear in early printed versions but were not recorded by those present.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/Before_the_Diet_of_Worms" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Wikisource, The World's Famous Orations (1906)</span></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>In May the Edict of Worms declared Luther an outlaw and banned his writings, but his protector, Frederick the Wise of Saxony, had him hidden at the Wartburg castle, where he translated the New Testament into German. The edict could not be enforced across the empire, and the Reformation spread until the Peace of Augsburg of 1555 allowed each prince to choose the religion of his lands.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-worms">Edict of Worms</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a></div></div></section>
